From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:50:34 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] hbbs: tell hjv-api a device's signed key, and keep Pro
 fields out of relay requests

hjv-api (hjviewer's server/api) adds a Windows PC to an account only with
the key hbbs knows for its ID, and checks the PC's signatures against that
key later. The loopback console gets the command it asks with:

  hjv-pk <id>   ->   HJVPK1 <id> <signed> <age>\n

<signed> is the base64 of the bytes get_pk puts into PunchHoleResponse and
RelayResponse: IdPk {id, pk} signed with hbbs's key, or "-" when hbbs knows
no key for the ID. <age> is the seconds since the peer last registered, or
"-" when not within the hour (loaded from the database, detached, or just
after a restart). The console has no authentication, and while hbbs
restarts any local program could take its port, so hjv-api takes a key
only with hbbs's signature over the ID it asked for. get_pk takes &mut self
and signs only for a client that sends its version, so the command calls it
on a clone with a version of its own. IDs unlike the ones hjv-api sends (1
to 64 letters, digits, '-' or '_') get no answer, as bad arguments of the
other commands get none, which also keeps every answer one short line. The
help lists the command.

RequestRelay reached the peer as the controller sent it, with the account
token, control permissions and controlled context that only a Pro server
sets: a hand-made controller could turn on permissions the PC's owner had
turned off. hbbs clears the three before it forwards the request.

tests/hjv_pk.rs starts hbbs with the key pair of hjv-api's own test vector
and checks the answer byte for byte against that vector and against the
PunchHoleResponse a controller gets; then after the peer disconnects, after
a restart (from the database), for IDs hjv-api never sends and input longer
than the console reads; and that a relay request reaches the peer without
the three fields and with everything else.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
